All right, the user’s main complaint is that the UI view is snapping oddly—specifically the input bar is unmounting during agent runs, causing a layout shift. I already addressed that by pinning the input component at the bottom and adding queueing logic so typed input isn’t lost mid‑run. Those changes were made in `src/components/Input.tsx` and some state variables were added in `src/tui.tsx` (the `streamBufRef` and a queue for pending renders). 

Now, after those layout fixes, there’s still a visual jitter when the model streams its output. The current `useEffect` in `tui.tsx` (the ticker) only updates the spinner, elapsed time, TPS, and TPS trend every 120 ms. The streaming output itself is rendered directly from the `stream` state, which gets updated on every token arrival via the tool‑block sentinel handling elsewhere. That means the UI repaints far more frequently than the ticker interval, causing flicker and, on fast generations, a noticeable “snapping” of the view as the output grows and pushes the input bar around.

Earlier I introduced a `streamBufRef` to accumulate the raw streaming text and a helper `visiblePrefix` (presumably defined in the same file) that extracts the portion of the buffer that should be displayed (e.g., trimming to the last N lines). The plan was to throttle the actual UI update of the `stream` state to the same 120 ms tick, so the UI only repaints ~8 times per second regardless of token rate. This will smooth out the rendering, keep the input bar anchored, and eliminate the layout flicker the user is seeing.

To implement this, I need to modify the existing ticker `useEffect`. The old code is:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (!running) return;
  const started = Date.now();
  const t = setInterval(() => {
    setSpin((s) => s + 1);
    setElapsed(Math.floor((Date.now() - started) / 1000));
    setTps(meterRef.current.tps());
    setTpsTrend(meterRef.current.trend());
  }, 120);
  return () => clearInterval(t);
}, [running]);
```

I’ll replace it with a version that, in addition to the existing state updates, also reads the current buffered stream (`streamBufRef.current`), runs it through `visiblePrefix` to get the visible slice, splits it into lines, keeps only the last 14 lines (to match the UI height constraints), and updates the `stream` state accordingly. Adding a brief comment will clarify why this extra work is here—namely, to throttle streaming updates and kill the layout flicker.

I must ensure the replacement string exactly matches the intended new code, preserving the surrounding structure and imports. The edit should be non‑replace‑all, targeting the specific `useEffect` block. The `old_string` will be the exact snippet shown above, and the `new_string` will be the expanded version with comments and the extra `setStream` call.

Thus, the next action is an `Edit` tool call on `src/tui.tsx`, swapping the old effect for the new throttled version. This directly addresses the remaining visual jitter while keeping the earlier layout fixes intact.

